= Semantics — Simple =

'''Semantics''' is about meaning.

A sentence has a form, but it also means something.

The form is its [[Syntax|syntax]].

The meaning is its semantics.

For example, a computer can check that a command is written in valid syntax while still needing rules that explain what the command should do.

Semantics also matters in ordinary language. Words can have several meanings, and context can change how a sentence is understood.

In logic and computing, people can define semantics very precisely. They say what symbols refer to, when statements are true, or what a program does.

This is important for [[Knowledge representation/Simple|knowledge representation]] because computers need more than labels. The relations and symbols must have defined meaning if software is going to reason with them.

